-- DB update 2026_09_05_35 -> 2026_09_05_36
--
-- Shape-based itemization (docs/itemization-phase-2.md), implementation order step 11: the real
-- per-item migration. Assigns primary_shape_id/secondary_shape_id/primary_share to all 13,455
-- already-assigned real items, replacing the step-5 placeholder (0/0/6000) that's sat on every
-- row since that migration. Also finalizes dps_delta for the 2,119 assigned real weapons,
-- superseding the "match original DPS" interim values from rev_1788605982752803359.sql now that
-- each weapon's real primary shape is known and the §7.7 caster-DPS-refund rule can actually
-- apply. budget_mult/stamina_delta/absorbed_spell_slots/armor_delta/block_value_delta are
-- unchanged from their current values -- this migration only touches primary/secondary shape
-- assignment and (for weapons) dps_delta.
--
-- *** TARGETS item_itemization -- same held-back batch as steps 5-7, 9's weapon fix. Do not let
-- this reach a worldserver restart / DB update before steps 5, 6, 7, and 9's weapon-fix file are
-- also present. This is the file that actually makes that batch safe to ship. ***
--
-- PRIMARY SHAPE ASSIGNMENT (mechanical, §9 steps 1-4):
-- For each item, read its current item_budget_template allocation. primary_share = sum of
-- Str/Agi/Int/Spirit/SpellPower/AP allocation, snapped to the nearest of {0, 4000, 6000, 8000,
-- 10000}. If that sum is 0 (or the item is a ring/neck, InventoryType 2/11, forced regardless of
-- its template per §9 step 6), primary_shape_id = 0 (the null/jewelry shape) and primary_share =
-- 0. Otherwise, the primary-stat sub-allocation is renormalized to ten-thousandths and matched
-- to the nearest of the 24 primary shapes by least-squares distance over the union of stat types
-- present in either the item or the candidate shape -- this is what "recovers dead budget"
-- automatically: an item that was 60% Str / 40% Defense renormalizes to 100% Str within the
-- primary side, so only Str's real 60% (snapped to primary_share 6000) is tested against the
-- shape catalog, and the Defense share simply isn't part of the primary sum at all, freeing that
-- 40% to the secondary side per §9 step 4.
--
-- Off-family real items (whose original mix doesn't correspond exactly to one of the 8 primary
-- stat-set families, e.g. old-conversion artifacts with Agi+Int+AttackPower all at once, a
-- combination no primary shape actually offers) get the closest available match rather than a
-- perfect one -- expected: consolidating ~13,455 distinct historical templates down to 24 shapes
-- cannot preserve every one exactly, that's the whole point of this refactor (§1).
--
-- SECONDARY SHAPE ASSIGNMENT ("by role and slot", §9 step 5) -- there is no stored "role" to
-- read, so it's inferred from the matched primary shape's stat set and the item's slot/subclass:
--   - Base pool: all 27 generic (rule-free) secondary shapes -- always eligible, per §6 "pair
--     with anything".
--   - + the 4 MP5-bearing shapes, if the primary stat set has no Spirit (MP5 forbids Spirit) --
--     this includes pure Int casters, physical Str/Agi items, and both hybrid families, NOT the
--     Spirit-bearing healer families (§7.4's regen-double-count rule structurally excludes MP5
--     from an item that already gets Spirit-derived regen).
--   - + the 3 Armor Pen-bearing shapes, if the primary stat set has Str or Agi.
--   - + the 11 Dodge/Parry/Block-bearing shapes, if the item is a shield (class 4, subclass 6)
--     OR (REVISED, third pass) its historical item_budget_template carried Defense, Dodge, Parry,
--     or Block (stats 12/13/14/15) directly. Defense is retired (§7.2, no shape-budget successor)
--     but Dodge/Parry/Block are NOT -- both were simply dropped by the original pass regardless,
--     since none of the four are primary stat types and so none were ever part of primary_sum --
--     discarding a real tank signal on 1,199 real items (weapons, armor, trinkets -- not just the
--     175 real shields). First broadened to cover Defense alone (998 items), then to the full set
--     after further review found entry 11931 "Dreadforge Retaliator" (58.8% Parry / 41.2% Crit,
--     no Defense at all) losing its Parry entirely -- the Defense-only version of this fix didn't
--     catch it. Preferred over the full eligible pool whenever eligible, not merely included in
--     it: picking uniformly from a ~30-45-shape pool would land on a tank-flavored shape only
--     rarely. An item whose primary shape is empty (see above) gets a nominal Str-primary
--     (Strength Solo, shape 22) purely so this rule's "requires Str" test can pass -- 145 real
--     items, not a real budget allocation (primary_share is untouched, still whatever it was,
--     usually 0) -- confirmed against entries 868 "Ardent Custodian" and 943 "Warden Staff", both
--     real Defense+Stamina-only weapons that previously landed on an arbitrary Crit/Haste/Mastery-
--     flavored secondary shape purely by `entry MOD pool_size` luck, with no way to ever reach a
--     tank shape at all. Any Dodge/Parry/Block-bearing shape still has to pass its own rule
--     (Dodge-only shapes accept Str or Agi; anything touching Parry or Block requires Str
--     specifically), so an Agi-only tank item (e.g. a feral druid weapon) is only eligible for the
--     Dodge-only subset.
-- Confirmed against the real catalog: many of the 175 real shields are NOT Str-primary in the
-- historical data (Spirit-only, Agi-only, even pure-Block-rating-with-zero-real-primary-stat
-- outliers) -- these correctly fall back to the generic-only or Armor-Pen-eligible pool rather
-- than being forced into tank flavor they'd fail validation for anyway.
--
-- Within the eligible pool for a given item, the specific shape is picked by `entry MOD
-- pool_size` against the pool sorted ascending by shape_id -- EXCEPT within the tank-preferred
-- pool above, where the item's real old weight on Dodge/Parry/Block plus whatever companion stat
-- the catalog offers (Crit/Haste/Mastery) is renormalized and matched to the *closest* tank shape
-- by squared distance (same nearest-neighbor method as primary-shape matching, step 3) -- picking
-- uniformly there would have let Dreadforge Retaliator's fix land it on, say, Block/Crit just as
-- easily as Parry/Crit, trading one real tank stat for another for no reason when the data says
-- which one it actually had. Defense itself is excluded from that match (it doesn't correspond to
-- any of the 11 tank shapes, so weighing it would only bias toward whichever shape has more
-- stats spread thinner, an artifact of squared distance, not a real preference) -- an item with
-- only Defense (no live Dodge/Parry/Block/Crit/Haste/Mastery weight, e.g. entries 868/943) has no
-- real signal for *which* tank shape to prefer, so it still falls back to the modulo pick.
-- Deterministic and reproducible either way (rerunning this migration's generator against the
-- same inputs reproduces the same output), not random, and not hand-curated per item.
--
-- One new tank shape needed to make that nearest-neighbor match honest: shape 70 "Parry/Block"
-- (rev_1788606546411907233.sql, revision 3) -- 11 real items historically carried Parry+Block
-- with no Dodge at all, and the 54-64 range had Dodge/Parry and Dodge/Block but not the plain
-- Parry/Block pair, so those 11 items would otherwise nearest-match to a shape carrying an
-- irrelevant Dodge weight instead. 46 secondary shapes total now, not 45.
--
-- WEAPON dps_delta (§7.7, REVISED): for each of the 2,119 assigned real weapons, using the refit,
-- hand-category-split item_weapon_dps_curve and item_weapon_dps_cost = 7.0
-- (rev_1788605982739495136.sql, rev_1788605573799930129.sql). item_weapon_dps_curve is now keyed
-- on (ilvl, quality, is_single_slot) rather than just (ilvl, quality): real single-slot weapons
-- (Two-Hand/Ranged/Thrown/Relic -- no off-hand/shield to also carry stats) run ~1.30x a
-- paired-slot weapon's (One-Hand/Main Hand/Off Hand) DPS at the same ilvl/quality, a real WotLK
-- itemization constant confirmed flat across the whole ilvl range -- pooling both into one curve
-- (the original version of this refit) made every real Two-Hand/Ranged/Thrown weapon look "above
-- curve" and cost budget it didn't have: with item_weapon_dps_cost = 7.0, a 2H weapon's typically
-- above-curve DPS routinely cost more than the item's *entire* stat budget, clamping
-- effective_budget to 0 and zeroing every stat on it. Confirmed against real data: entries 7723
-- "Mograine's Might", 9413 "The Rockpounder", 11921 "Impervious Giant" (all Two-Hand maces) had
-- every stat but Stamina zeroed under the pooled curve; 317 of 651 assigned Two-Hand weapons hit
-- the same wall overall (plus most Thrown, some Ranged), vs. 0 of the One-Hand/Main Hand/Off Hand
-- weapons. The split curve cuts the number of weapons whose entire stat budget gets consumed by
-- their own DPS deviation from 377 to 61 (residual cases are ilvl-thin budgets meeting real,
-- still-above-category-median DPS -- itemization scatter, not a systemic miscalibration).
--
-- The "primary shape contains Str or Agi" test that gated this in
-- the original pass is wrong for weapons whose primary_stat_set is EMPTY (shape 0): an empty
-- primary_stat_set means the OLD item_budget_template put 100% of its weight on secondary stats
-- (Defense/Dodge/Parry/Block/Stamina) with ZERO weight on Str/Agi/Int/Spirit/SP/AP -- a genuine
-- caster weapon's old template always carried real Int/Spirit/SP weight (that's what lands it in
-- shapes 1-9 below), so an empty set is a physical/tank weapon misrouted through the null shape,
-- not a caster one. Confirmed against real data: entries 868 "Ardent Custodian" and 943 "Warden
-- Staff" (a druid feral-tank staff) both had old stats of Defense+Stamina only and were incorrectly
-- getting a full DPS refund under the original pass.
--   - if the matched primary shape is non-empty and contains Str or Agi (a melee/physical
--     weapon), OR the matched primary shape is empty (shape 0 -- also physical, see above):
--     dps_delta is set so WeaponDpsCurve(ilvl, quality) + dps_delta reproduces that weapon's
--     ORIGINAL Blizzard DPS as closely as possible, pulled from the pristine base dump
--     (data/sql/base/db_world/item_template.sql) for the same reason the interim pass needed it --
--     Phase 5's earlier conversion already overwrote dmg_min1/dmg_max1 for many of these weapons
--     in place, so live item_template is not a reliable source of "what this weapon originally
--     was" (confirmed: Bonereaver's Edge, entry 17076, is 206/310 in the base dump but
--     180.594/335.39 live).
--   - if the matched primary shape is non-empty and contains NEITHER Str nor Agi (a genuine
--     caster weapon -- shapes 1-9): dps_delta = -0.20 * baseline, a PARTIAL refund (was a full
--     refund to 0 DPS in the original pass -- corrected per user feedback: a caster weapon should
--     only lose 20% of its DPS, not all of it). The refunded 20% (baseline * 0.20 * 7.0) flows
--     into that weapon's shape-driven stat allocation instead, per §7.7's rule.
--   - if a physical weapon (either branch above) has no usable original-DPS data -- 39 real
--     entries have dmg_min1 = dmg_max1 = 0 even in the pristine base dump (e.g. entries
--     20003/20005 "Devilsaur Claws", a real Agi weapon, which the original pass's fallback of
--     `dps_delta = -baseline` had zeroed out entirely, indistinguishable in effect from a caster
--     refund even though its primary shape was correctly Agility all along): dps_delta = 0,
--     falling back to the curve's own baseline DPS for that (ilvl, quality) rather than forcing 0.
--     No weapon, physical or caster, should ever end up at exactly 0 final DPS.
--
-- Out of scope: the 398 real Rare/Epic weapons with no item_itemization row at all (no existing
-- template to read a primary split from -- a different task, not a migration of existing data).
--
